13:11.252 --> 13:15.509
that we have a common language when it comes to what are the impact

13:15.589 --> 13:18.545
or how we're going to measure the impact, the service levels,

13:18.722 --> 13:22.160
the metrics. That we'll be talking about so that we are on the same page

13:22.273 --> 13:25.390
when it comes to that topic. And then the good

13:25.406 --> 13:28.828
thing about this is communicating and measuring our

13:28.860 --> 13:32.523
impact will help us tell the, the story

13:32.555 --> 13:36.171
to the community of the things that the city is doing when it

13:36.671 --> 13:40.300
comes to, of course, improving the quality of life and making

13:40.332 --> 13:44.220
sure that our city is attractive to live— for

13:44.284 --> 13:47.917
residents to live, play, and work. Any questions on

13:48.417 --> 13:52.821
this? Just key planning objectives for this budget planning.

13:55.100 --> 13:58.375
Awesome. All right,

13:58.536 --> 14:01.972
so the next few slides I'm

14:02.472 --> 14:05.425
gonna be not nerding out, but I just want to cover some of the things

14:05.538 --> 14:08.944
when it comes to the budget in general. It might be a review

14:08.992 --> 14:12.302
for you, but I think it's a good way to just think about the

14:12.802 --> 14:15.748
budget. Every year, the city is required to,

14:16.789 --> 14:19.449
to plan for its budget and to balance its budget.

14:19.834 --> 14:23.487
But ideally, what's, what's the budget is really

14:23.599 --> 14:27.413
all about? And I see the budget has this potential

14:28.086 --> 14:31.500
of acting and being a tool in these areas.

14:32.397 --> 14:36.131
The budget's full potential, it helps us guide what we do

14:36.659 --> 14:39.626
and how we do it as a city. I see the budget,

14:40.011 --> 14:43.123
and, um, when I was in my MPA program, we've been—

14:43.460 --> 14:46.749
we've been taught that the budget is one of the most

14:47.249 --> 14:50.969
important policy documents that a government could establish

14:51.177 --> 14:55.124
and present or publish. And I believe

14:55.624 --> 14:58.927
that that is correct. Like, as a local government, the budget is

14:59.119 --> 15:02.857
the most important policy decision and guidelines because

15:03.467 --> 15:06.584
it It's that where you allocate the resources that you want,

15:06.664 --> 15:11.067
that you have, to the needs that you think that the community need

15:11.567 --> 15:15.630
at this point. The budget as a policy guide

15:15.662 --> 15:19.567
helps the community understand where the priorities of the council

15:19.760 --> 15:23.311
are, and then it helps give direction to the staff

15:23.600 --> 15:28.581
where those priorities and policy should be focused

15:28.661 --> 15:31.966
on. You're familiar with the tool of a

15:32.466 --> 15:36.037
budget as a financial plan. We're just not just— it's not about just

15:36.117 --> 15:39.274
what we're going to be spending the next fiscal year, but we're also going to

15:39.322 --> 15:42.816
be looking at our revenues, making sure that what we collect,

15:42.864 --> 15:46.679
what we bring in, it's the same amount of money that we can spend on,

15:46.711 --> 15:51.391
and we don't create any structural imbalance

15:51.439 --> 15:54.500
when it comes to the budget. Something that

15:55.000 --> 15:58.491
is very interesting to me is looking at the budget as a communication tool.

15:59.200 --> 16:02.847
The Government Financial Officers, or GFOA,

16:03.896 --> 16:07.688
this— they have recently changed their budget criteria when it

16:08.188 --> 16:11.775
comes to publishing budget books for the cities. They've been pushing

16:12.033 --> 16:15.232
the cities and local governments and school districts and all

16:15.345 --> 16:19.030
those entities, the public entities, to use the budget as

16:19.078 --> 16:22.243
a communication tool. One of the changes

16:22.275 --> 16:25.664
that they would like to implement is making sure that the budget

16:25.793 --> 16:29.099
acts like as a communication tool when it comes to

16:29.115 --> 16:33.325
the focus— that focuses on the public,

16:33.630 --> 16:37.072
the elected officials, and the residents as the target audience of the

16:37.572 --> 16:40.785
information. I know sometimes it's really hard to read 300,

16:40.801 --> 16:44.449
400 pages of budget, but that's something that

16:45.079 --> 16:48.043
we're planning to, um, to improve,

16:48.464 --> 16:52.174
like how we can communicate or use that budget as a communication

16:52.191 --> 16:56.995
tool and be strategic way of communicating consistently

16:58.229 --> 17:01.482
that will help both you, the elected official, and the public

17:01.594 --> 17:05.488
understand more our city budget and our financial condition.

17:06.706 --> 17:09.366
The budget is an operation guide.

17:10.648 --> 17:14.783
As you approve the budget, it sets the boundary of

17:14.799 --> 17:18.308
how much workload the departments will work on.

17:18.677 --> 17:23.143
It's sets the, um, the expectations of

17:23.240 --> 17:26.904
like what the city should be able to accomplish in the next fiscal year.

17:26.968 --> 17:30.062
So it helps guide that oper— um, guide the

17:30.562 --> 17:31.395
staff operationally.

17:33.489 --> 17:37.058
Also, the budget, I see it as a system. Um, as you can

17:37.139 --> 17:40.951
see from here, um, in the previous

17:41.451 --> 17:44.021
meetings, you guys have the opportunity to work on your strategic plan.

17:45.093 --> 17:48.706
Um, the strategic plan that you guys are working will help set the

17:49.206 --> 17:52.370
community priorities. And those community priorities

17:52.563 --> 17:55.921
can be translated and will be tied back to the budget,

17:55.937 --> 17:59.761
because where your priorities are, that's where we try to put resources

17:59.954 --> 18:03.199
on. The budget is influenced

18:03.247 --> 18:05.625
by the capital improvement plan.

18:06.461 --> 18:10.124
We try to review those infrastructure projects that will come online

18:10.317 --> 18:14.066
and the current projects that we're working on, and all those planning

18:14.164 --> 18:17.625
behind those capital improvement plans do tie back

18:18.125 --> 18:22.231
to the budget. The master plans and general

18:22.247 --> 18:24.465
plans and all those things, um,

18:26.025 --> 18:28.985
in— based on your policy decision,

18:29.259 --> 18:33.213
could impact what we include, what we don't include, what we budget in

18:33.713 --> 18:37.392
our, in our fiscal year. The budget that

18:37.892 --> 18:41.303
we're hoping to achieve, hopefully this coming planning is that

18:41.447 --> 18:45.365
to emphasize more on long-term financial planning, we would

18:45.865 --> 18:49.154
like to develop some financial model that

18:49.654 --> 18:53.426
we can share with the council when it comes to the revenues and

18:53.490 --> 18:57.102
the expenditures, the staffing, the different projects from the

18:57.602 --> 19:01.277
CIP, the different programs that the different departments

19:01.777 --> 19:04.890
are thinking about, that we want to put it in a model like what we

19:05.390 --> 19:08.604
did when when it comes to the utility rates study that we did,

19:08.668 --> 19:11.932
wherein you're able to see the trends of where we are and where the

19:12.432 --> 19:15.674
needs are. And if we see some deficit, for example,

19:16.705 --> 19:20.300
that we will have enough time to react, that we will have enough time to

19:20.574 --> 19:23.395
pivot and make changes to the budget.

19:25.334 --> 19:29.665
The last 2 is— it's more on the budget monitoring

19:29.778 --> 19:33.113
and communication, wherein as we set up these

19:33.722 --> 19:37.572
service levels and figure out how we can measure the

19:38.072 --> 19:41.342
success of those service levels and where we are.

19:41.967 --> 19:45.304
We can monitor that through establishing performance measures.

19:45.817 --> 19:50.276
And also, as part of our year-end auditing,

19:50.934 --> 19:54.720
the Act for— will act as a reporting tool for the year-end

19:54.800 --> 19:58.297
as part of what happened in that fiscal year. Any question between

19:58.329 --> 20:02.020
these ideas of budget as a tool in the last

20:02.520 --> 20:06.067
slides, and then budget as a system, like how everything kind of ties back to

20:06.567 --> 20:09.727
the budget and why— the reason why we're spending this time talking about the budget.

20:10.662 --> 20:11.322
Nope? Okay.

20:14.835 --> 20:18.124
Now we're gonna go over about the roles in the budget process.

20:19.688 --> 20:23.851
So here's some of the key stakeholders.

20:24.061 --> 20:27.600
Of course, we have the residents as well as a stakeholder when it comes to

20:28.100 --> 20:31.767
the budget process. And I'll talk about where they can put their input later

20:31.863 --> 20:36.054
on. But in terms of like the process itself and the timeline

20:36.554 --> 20:40.501
and how we discuss things, here's some of the general area of

20:40.614 --> 20:44.018
like responsibilities. The very first

20:44.066 --> 20:47.438
one is for the City Council is to, of course,

20:47.518 --> 20:51.131
establish community priorities and policy direction, which we

20:51.693 --> 20:55.995
talked about that earlier. Those really policy decisions

20:56.171 --> 20:59.861
and direction is what we take as staff, which we'll

20:59.877 --> 21:03.022
go over between, uh, for the mayor and the city manager.

21:03.631 --> 21:07.706
Um, the next one is providing guidance on service levels

21:07.899 --> 21:11.476
and the desired outcomes that you want as a council, as a body,

21:11.894 --> 21:15.183
when it comes to, to what level of service

21:15.215 --> 21:17.814
are we comfortable to provide for to our community.

21:18.889 --> 21:22.065
And then, um, As part of the tentative budget process,

21:22.322 --> 21:25.706
you know, first meeting in May, usually that's the

21:26.206 --> 21:29.332
official time wherein the administration, mayor and city manager,

21:29.637 --> 21:33.166
will present the tentative budget to you guys. And that's

21:33.666 --> 21:37.385
the time that you have to review, deliberate, and of course later on in June,

21:37.658 --> 21:40.641
adopt the budget. But even though that's in May,

21:41.444 --> 21:44.331
today is October. We already starting to,

21:44.877 --> 21:48.228
to do some budget discussion because we want to involve the council and

21:48.728 --> 21:52.731
we want to have that discussion that before we come to that point of tentative

21:53.231 --> 21:56.865
budget presentation, that you guys are in the loop of all the discussions

21:56.977 --> 22:00.070
that we have and how we came to be to that, um,

22:00.342 --> 22:03.435
to that point by that time that the budget will be presented to

22:03.935 --> 22:07.009
you guys. Next is the mayor and the city manager.

22:08.291 --> 22:11.800
Their responsibility, of course, is to translate those council

22:12.300 --> 22:16.062
priorities and policy direction from the council that will trickle down to

22:16.110 --> 22:20.453
the staff. Of course, the responsibility to make

22:20.953 --> 22:24.747
sure that we balance the citywide needs versus

22:24.924 --> 22:28.673
the available resources that we have. And as part of that tentative

22:28.705 --> 22:33.416
budget presentation is to present that recommended budget for council

22:33.916 --> 22:37.486
consideration by that time. Next is

22:37.919 --> 22:42.137
finance and the budget team. So our responsibility

22:42.169 --> 22:45.925
for you guys is to help establish the budget process,

22:45.941 --> 22:49.487
the guidelines, the timelines, and of course to develop

22:49.987 --> 22:53.146
some financial assumptions and analyze some of the budget requests

22:53.646 --> 22:57.383
that we will be receiving. And then our hope is that we can provide

22:57.399 --> 23:01.876
some objective analysis and options for you and recommendation

23:02.085 --> 23:06.097
to the body as the time comes that you guys consider

23:06.944 --> 23:09.875
what decisions you would like to make when it comes to the budget.

23:10.809 --> 23:15.287
The departments, their role and responsibility primarily

23:15.625 --> 23:19.216
are heavily involved with identifying the operational and the

23:19.716 --> 23:22.402
service needs within their area, within the city.

23:22.920 --> 23:26.040
And then of course they, they can hear that from the residents as well.

23:27.689 --> 23:31.852
