01:14:23.108 --> 01:14:26.609
So I'll try and kind of come back and restate it. Knowing that there are

01:14:27.109 --> 01:14:30.398
going to be some additional METs and some property tax values that

01:14:30.898 --> 01:14:33.690
are going to go up, I don't think that's difficult. Look, even a layman like

01:14:34.190 --> 01:14:37.302
me can project that over the next couple of years. If, if, if— here's the

01:14:37.802 --> 01:14:41.686
if— if we can anticipate when the building is completed

01:14:42.280 --> 01:14:45.218
and when those taxes are going to be collected, we know the rates are going

01:14:45.234 --> 01:14:48.108
to be collected at today's rate. That's fine. We can project that.

01:14:49.747 --> 01:14:53.363
That's information that we haven't had, that I haven't had,

01:14:53.684 --> 01:14:56.962
and that has been very vague, even when we've

01:14:56.978 --> 01:15:00.176
asked for it. That's something I'm looking for in this year's budget,

01:15:00.256 --> 01:15:03.582
is some more clarity in what are the projected revenues that we

01:15:04.082 --> 01:15:07.150
know will come over the next 4 or 5 years.

01:15:08.130 --> 01:15:11.842
Now, can it be impeded? Can it change? Can something disrupt

01:15:11.906 --> 01:15:15.582
that? Absolutely. But if we can look at it and say, look, as long as

01:15:15.614 --> 01:15:18.756
things continue on this trajectory, there's likely to be

01:15:19.256 --> 01:15:22.652
some of these sources, and perhaps as we get into that

01:15:23.152 --> 01:15:26.675
1 or 2-year cycle where we're actually doing the planning, we can say,

01:15:26.740 --> 01:15:30.347
well, we expect this revenue source to grow by this amount. We can't

01:15:30.847 --> 01:15:33.841
really spend it or budget it yet, but we can look at it and say,

01:15:34.450 --> 01:15:37.464
maybe we can get to some of these projects when that revenue comes.

01:15:37.817 --> 01:15:41.249
That's the key. I'm good with that. I think—

01:15:41.749 --> 01:15:46.066
You can't spend it— like, I can't say I'm

01:15:46.566 --> 01:15:49.293
going to do all these things in the budget this year because we're hoping this

01:15:49.793 --> 01:15:53.275
will happen this year. But we can certainly forecast, and it is—

01:15:53.387 --> 01:15:57.385
we completely understand that. A forecast is a forecast,

01:15:57.545 --> 01:16:00.756
and it is based on all the information we have today, and it's

01:16:00.837 --> 01:16:04.770
okay that tomorrow I have new information that changes that forecast.

01:16:06.280 --> 01:16:09.732
Really what's being asked, and I've heard it 1,000 times,

01:16:09.765 --> 01:16:12.929
so I'll say it differently, is how often do we sit up here and say,

01:16:13.442 --> 01:16:16.349
oh, let's use RAP tax for that, or let's use it, and we spent,

01:16:16.430 --> 01:16:19.144
like, we're talking about a city hall, and we're talking about all these things,

01:16:19.160 --> 01:16:23.159
and oh, we're going to use MET for that. Well, we've spent year 2028

01:16:23.271 --> 01:16:26.467
MET 15 times. We don't have that much MET.

01:16:26.548 --> 01:16:29.904
We only have this much MET. So we're trying to decide, well, every time we

01:16:30.404 --> 01:16:34.190
turn around, well, how much is it projected that in 2028

01:16:34.690 --> 01:16:37.749
or 2029 that number might be so that we can plan,

01:16:38.342 --> 01:16:41.500
okay, well, that's the year that we could do the City Hall,

01:16:41.612 --> 01:16:44.561
or that's the year that we could do this project. Because in the meantime,

01:16:44.578 --> 01:16:47.303
we just keep saying, yeah, do it, we'll do it with MET, and we don't

01:16:47.803 --> 01:16:50.749
know what MET even equals. So is that what you're asking? Yeah, because it doesn't

01:16:50.765 --> 01:16:55.975
really give us— it doesn't give us— how do

01:16:56.475 --> 01:16:59.598
I say this? It's like, anyway, I'm probably not going to give it a good

01:17:00.098 --> 01:17:03.195
example, so I won't try. Um, but, but for me as a

01:17:03.211 --> 01:17:06.583
decision maker, it's just helpful to look forward to—

01:17:07.514 --> 01:17:11.143
I know, and look, and I understand expenses can change, right? Our costs go

01:17:11.643 --> 01:17:15.382
up. So yeah, we may, we may collect this. Look what happened with our sheriff's

01:17:15.882 --> 01:17:18.529
contract, and look what will probably happen next year with our sheriff's contract and with

01:17:19.029 --> 01:17:21.885
our personnel decisions. I mean, that's part of why we need to weigh all that,

01:17:21.901 --> 01:17:23.908
and those resources have to come from somewhere.

01:17:25.594 --> 01:17:29.531
Anyway, I hope you understand

01:17:30.031 --> 01:17:33.001
what I'm asking for. I don't want to change your mindset on how you budget,

01:17:33.017 --> 01:17:36.649
because you're a great budgeter, Kimberly, and I'm glad you're on our team,

01:17:36.761 --> 01:17:40.248
because you keep us out of trouble. But for me,

01:17:40.296 --> 01:17:43.687
it's really about seeing into the future, or at least looking at

01:17:44.187 --> 01:17:48.780
it and saying, this is probable, and if these

01:17:48.812 --> 01:17:52.074
probabilities come into play, We don't have to spend them,

01:17:52.187 --> 01:17:55.369
but we need to know that they're going to be available to perhaps start to

01:17:55.385 --> 01:17:58.856
touch some of these things that we just can't get to, that we think

01:17:59.356 --> 01:18:02.334
we can get to, but we can't because we don't know how to get to

01:18:02.834 --> 01:18:06.349
the— to get the budget, to get the funding put together to be

01:18:06.849 --> 01:18:11.136
able to afford them. I think

01:18:11.636 --> 01:18:14.730
part of that, as we look forward to that also, and this can

01:18:15.230 --> 01:18:17.815
take Evan as well because I think he's worked on this, is, uh,

01:18:19.332 --> 01:18:22.430
yeah, we're going to get MET, but what's our taxable value projected to be next

01:18:22.930 --> 01:18:26.205
year? We know when— what's coming, what's approved, what's got a

01:18:26.270 --> 01:18:29.755
building permit, and what will finish by January 1st. We know what last

01:18:30.255 --> 01:18:33.952
year's taxable value was. What do we suspect next year's taxable value?

01:18:34.355 --> 01:18:37.933
And if some of these projects, like say, uh, the Facebook

01:18:38.433 --> 01:18:41.328
expansion or Meta expansion, is a 3-year expansion— Williams project.

01:18:41.815 --> 01:18:44.544
Or Williams project's set to be finished in 2029,

01:18:45.058 --> 01:18:48.154
what's the value going to add in 2029?

01:18:48.572 --> 01:18:51.829
What's the value add at this rate so that we can say, okay,

01:18:52.519 --> 01:18:56.819
at that rate, you know, it'll help with the code that Council

01:18:56.835 --> 01:19:00.237
Member Hirsch put together on public safety. If we know that, that our value

01:19:00.366 --> 01:19:03.767
add or our taxable value is here and we need to maintain

01:19:04.267 --> 01:19:07.570
85%, what do we need to do this year so we don't in 20 years

01:19:08.084 --> 01:19:11.325
get to a point where we're out of whack because we know what our Yeah,

01:19:11.518 --> 01:19:15.083
I'm going to give you an example. It's like this. It's like me

01:19:15.583 --> 01:19:17.844
knowing that I'm going to get a raise or that my salary is going to

01:19:17.877 --> 01:19:20.927
increase next year at the end of the year, but I'm not willing to share

01:19:21.427 --> 01:19:24.845
that with my wife yet because I'm worried she's going to spend it or put

01:19:25.345 --> 01:19:27.848
it somewhere where she shouldn't. And my wife's not the spender in our family,

01:19:27.912 --> 01:19:31.862
by the way, so she will— She's not

01:19:32.231 --> 01:19:34.913
going to be the one to blame on that, okay?

01:19:36.342 --> 01:19:39.535
That's what I'm talking about, is I don't want that

01:19:40.035 --> 01:19:43.868
information kept from us. I want to know that, okay, look, the boss has

01:19:43.884 --> 01:19:47.656
already said, and it's likely that you're— now, can something change? Can market conditions

01:19:48.156 --> 01:19:51.587
change? Can ownership of the company change, and the guy that made me the

01:19:52.087 --> 01:19:55.359
promise is no longer? Sure, those things can happen. But if we continue to

01:19:55.859 --> 01:19:58.536
march in the direction that we are, what are the things that we can reasonably

01:19:59.130 --> 01:20:03.110
count on? It's— Can we at least examine that As

01:20:03.286 --> 01:20:06.686
we're looking forward, what that'll do for me is

01:20:06.750 --> 01:20:10.279
give me at least some hope that some of these things that we talk about

01:20:10.779 --> 01:20:13.582
perpetually, we'll be able to get to at some point in time.

01:20:13.727 --> 01:20:16.645
And to start to really timeline that and say,

01:20:17.078 --> 01:20:20.206
this may not be something that will even happen while I'm in office, I'm okay

01:20:20.706 --> 01:20:24.295
with that. As long as we've set a path towards that outcome,

01:20:24.375 --> 01:20:27.679
that makes me, that makes me whole. I think what

01:20:28.179 --> 01:20:31.548
you're seeing is the plan that we've had to try to

01:20:31.933 --> 01:20:35.353
mature our processes along the way, this is

01:20:35.385 --> 01:20:38.645
the continuation. So if you recall, like, this is

01:20:38.741 --> 01:20:42.546
the first year we have a full-time budget manager

01:20:42.675 --> 01:20:46.593
that is overseeing the budget process, right? Everything from

01:20:46.625 --> 01:20:49.403
every year prior to that is just try to fit in with all the things

01:20:49.419 --> 01:20:52.951
that everybody else is already doing, right? And when we were doing our

01:20:53.451 --> 01:20:57.527
long-range forecasting, Up to this point, the focus is on those heavy big-ticket

01:20:58.027 --> 01:21:01.397
items, which is getting our capital improvement plan built out.

01:21:01.862 --> 01:21:05.459
We've got a pretty strong 5-year. Yeah. We're moving it out to 10 years,

01:21:05.716 --> 01:21:08.895
even on the capital planning side, in terms of being able to

01:21:08.927 --> 01:21:12.123
see what's coming down the pipe from that. And then

01:21:12.155 --> 01:21:15.944
the second phase to that is, are we ensuring that we're incorporating

01:21:15.992 --> 01:21:19.685
those operational costs that come along with implementing these infrastructure projects?

01:21:19.750 --> 01:21:23.282
projects, and that is part of your operational forecasting

01:21:23.459 --> 01:21:27.248
out as well. So you're— I want to clarify that we haven't kept

01:21:27.748 --> 01:21:31.423
anything from you. What you haven't seen is because we haven't

01:21:31.923 --> 01:21:35.597
done it, right? We haven't looked at the operational side. I probably misstated that,

01:21:35.629 --> 01:21:39.323
Kimberly. I'll take that back and clear it up. I don't feel like you're withholding

01:21:39.371 --> 01:21:41.683
stuff from me, but I don't feel like I have the information I need either.

01:21:41.763 --> 01:21:44.830
Right. And that is where you've seen us move to, okay,

01:21:45.392 --> 01:21:48.475
We're first trying to work on our 5-year needs assessment. We're moving,

01:21:48.668 --> 01:21:52.619
and with that comes a 5-year revenue forecast for how you fulfill that.

01:21:53.085 --> 01:21:56.843
What are these things that help make those long-term decisions?

01:21:57.035 --> 01:22:00.649
And then particularly with your capital outlay repair and replacement,

01:22:01.291 --> 01:22:04.744
we will get to the point where you will have a 40-year forecast for

01:22:05.244 --> 01:22:09.048
what you anticipate future expenditures, and those

01:22:09.096 --> 01:22:12.035
are essential for ensuring your rates are sufficient.

01:22:12.533 --> 01:22:15.966
to save up for what those big-ticket items are. I won't

01:22:16.466 --> 01:22:19.560
be here in 40 years, so I don't anticipate seeing that outcome. But those

01:22:19.656 --> 01:22:22.704
things are important to our process improvement. So,

01:22:23.586 --> 01:22:27.180
okay, Councilmember Hewish wanted to jump in. Let's give him a chance for

01:22:27.680 --> 01:22:31.399
a second. Yeah, I just want to second what, what Councilmember Wright's talking about.

01:22:31.448 --> 01:22:34.704
Just a basic 5-year pro forma that

01:22:35.218 --> 01:22:38.763
is a big picture. It will obviously need to adapt and

01:22:39.263 --> 01:22:43.032
get tighter and change year to year as Those revenues and

01:22:43.532 --> 01:22:47.162
expenses come closer to us, but kind of mapping out the next 5 years,

01:22:47.258 --> 01:22:51.291
the IFFPs will probably

01:22:51.791 --> 01:22:55.999
be on a rolling 5-year number. So even though they're in a 6-year payment

01:22:56.015 --> 01:22:59.148
format, a rolling 5-year of what does that look like in the

01:22:59.648 --> 01:23:02.157
next 5 years and what roads do we need to build in the next 5

01:23:02.657 --> 01:23:07.142
years, I think it will help us to not

01:23:07.642 --> 01:23:11.095
put off the big projects that need to be paid for. It's really easy for

01:23:11.595 --> 01:23:14.775
us to jump on the little small low-hanging fruit. But when

01:23:14.872 --> 01:23:17.856
we do that and we kind of lose sight of the big picture, we end

01:23:18.356 --> 01:23:19.986
up not doing the big projects that need to get done.

01:23:22.225 --> 01:23:25.522
And one thing I wanted to just kind of get maybe as a report,

01:23:25.780 --> 01:23:28.382
some of you may not know this, but I was with Speaker Schultz last night

01:23:28.882 --> 01:23:32.687
at a listening tour activity thing for

01:23:32.703 --> 01:23:36.158
a few hours, and it was kind of fun just to talk about where

01:23:36.658 --> 01:23:39.692
the state's at. But when we go up there and we're asking for roads and

01:23:40.192 --> 01:23:43.178
we're asking for help from the state to do whatever it is that we're doing.

01:23:44.078 --> 01:23:47.610
Last night, some of the questions that I got asked for

01:23:48.110 --> 01:23:50.805
us to evaluate, because they're saying, well, if you're not doing this, we're not doing

01:23:50.821 --> 01:23:54.112
that type of a conversation, is, well,

01:23:54.209 --> 01:23:56.601
what are your water rates at? You might have to raise your water rates.

01:23:56.697 --> 01:23:58.784
And I says, our water rates are good. I just looked at them. And he

01:23:59.284 --> 01:24:02.606
said, okay, good. What are your impact fees at? Because right now they're around

01:24:02.638 --> 01:24:06.523
$30,000. I said, I think we were just a little under— well, our building permit

01:24:07.023 --> 01:24:10.722
fees these, including impact. But I said, but with what we passed on Tuesday,

01:24:11.138 --> 01:24:14.199
I think we're going to be closer to 40,000, is the number we were talking

01:24:14.699 --> 01:24:17.035
about. But he was asking, you know, have you guys evaluated this? And I said,

01:24:17.131 --> 01:24:21.634
yes, yes, yes. Because I checked those boxes and said we're

01:24:22.134 --> 01:24:26.169
above average or we're at this mark, we've already— It absolutely

01:24:27.082 --> 01:24:30.111
made it to where it's like, okay, then now it's time that if you've done

01:24:30.611 --> 01:24:33.867
all this, we can step in. But that, that's just how they're evaluating

01:24:33.883 --> 01:24:37.051
it too, are we doing our job? And I know that's a little bit of

01:24:37.551 --> 01:24:40.833
a report and a tangent, but kind of on what we're talking about, this 5-year

01:24:41.333 --> 01:24:42.596
plan and our IFFPs and that.

01:24:44.634 --> 01:24:47.820
So that's crucial to us going where we're at.

01:24:49.324 --> 01:24:53.206
I think what's important about the master plan concept is that with

01:24:53.610 --> 01:24:56.585
the master plan, and I don't mean to get— I'm not gonna get sidetracked too

01:24:57.085 --> 01:25:00.127
far, but with the master plan, you're projecting this window

01:25:00.627 --> 01:25:04.476
and this target, right? And we've been going at a fast pace. And so

01:25:04.557 --> 01:25:07.911
what we don't want to miss is we don't want to miss the growth that's

01:25:08.411 --> 01:25:13.994
happening in that fast pace. And we're trying to collect for it. But so

01:25:14.892 --> 01:25:18.343
you ramp it up and you build this schedule that you're preparing so that you

01:25:18.843 --> 01:25:22.195
can supply that level of service as growth comes in. But what that doesn't

01:25:22.211 --> 01:25:26.063
mean is we can do that, but it's going to still be

01:25:26.563 --> 01:25:30.506
dependent on how many homes come in in that next given Like, our impact

01:25:30.538 --> 01:25:33.551
fees could be down, and we need to be able to

01:25:33.599 --> 01:25:37.669
prepare and fluctuate for that too. So just because you raise it doesn't

01:25:37.749 --> 01:25:41.579
mean that that means we have more money. It's still dependent on

01:25:41.691 --> 01:25:45.185
the factors. You're absolutely right. It could have and will have

01:25:45.233 --> 01:25:48.678
a chilling effect on the market in general, just like we

01:25:48.726 --> 01:25:52.412
already have a chilling effect from interest rates and from

01:25:52.912 --> 01:25:56.261
the lack of city sale of water. Hey, so real

01:25:56.341 --> 01:25:59.599
quick, thanks for being patient. Melissa, would you jump in?

01:26:00.706 --> 01:26:05.040
Thank you. 12 comments later. Ladies last,

01:26:05.168 --> 01:26:08.635
right? Sorry. Remember, I had twins. Anyway, Kimberly,

01:26:09.373 --> 01:26:13.128
I really liked how you said mature in our process, because I really think

01:26:13.628 --> 01:26:16.178
that's what it is. Like, we're a grown-up city now, and we're going to act

01:26:16.678 --> 01:26:20.061
like grown-ups. Anyway, like, I think that it's

01:26:20.174 --> 01:26:23.401
not I've got a bunch of money that Grandma and Grandpa sent me, and it's

01:26:23.901 --> 01:26:25.842
burning a hole in my pocket, and I need to go spend it at the

01:26:26.195 --> 01:26:30.273
gas station. I really like the concept of saving and planning

01:26:30.289 --> 01:26:33.501
and forecasting. It's really

01:26:34.001 --> 01:26:36.375
super frustrating, both for residents and elected officials,

01:26:37.033 --> 01:26:40.614
when they talk to us about a road connection that they want, a turn

01:26:40.662 --> 01:26:44.258
lane, a stoplight, something like that, and we say to them, oh,

01:26:44.740 --> 01:26:48.480
we can't afford it right now. And Councilmember Wright knows that we've

01:26:48.528 --> 01:26:51.898
been like really railed by saying like,

01:26:51.963 --> 01:26:54.867
well, how are we going to pay for it? And they really let us know

01:26:54.948 --> 01:26:57.949
that that's not an acceptable answer. So being able to say,

01:26:58.462 --> 01:27:01.848
hey, if things happen the way we're

01:27:01.929 --> 01:27:05.427
projecting them to be, this is when we think that we'll

01:27:05.459 --> 01:27:09.616
possibly be able to do this. And that was the other thing. I think

01:27:10.354 --> 01:27:13.692
the word forecast or projection should be used

01:27:13.708 --> 01:27:17.546
when we have this, whatever we end up creating, instead of the

01:27:17.578 --> 01:27:20.597
word plan. Because to me, it's not a plan.

01:27:20.709 --> 01:27:24.450
People will look at it and go, you didn't get this well, 3 years,

01:27:24.707 --> 01:27:28.224
you know, whatever. And if we can say forecast, it just helps people

01:27:28.256 --> 01:27:31.515
understand over and over and over again. The weather

01:27:31.547 --> 01:27:35.545
might change. Yeah, the weather might change. So anyway, I concur

01:27:36.045 --> 01:27:37.472
with everything that's been said. I really appreciate it.

01:27:40.601 --> 01:27:43.792
Great discussion. That's why we're going to spending a day of

01:27:43.968 --> 01:27:47.561
revenue discussion. But yeah, overall great discussions,

01:27:47.673 --> 01:27:51.281
and I don't need to explain the connection between budget process and long-term

01:27:51.346 --> 01:27:53.815
financial planning. I think we're on the same page on that one.

01:27:54.890 --> 01:27:58.386
All right, just a few more slides, and, um, this will

01:27:58.418 --> 01:28:01.193
be the Budget Congress, um, that I'm talking to,

01:28:01.930 --> 01:28:05.412
um, earlier. That will be a day event.

01:28:05.653 --> 01:28:09.715
So the purpose of the Budget Congress is to give departments an opportunity

01:28:09.731 --> 01:28:13.007
to tell their story and help leadership— that's

01:28:13.507 --> 01:28:17.486
council and administration— make informed and strategic budget decisions.

01:28:17.599 --> 01:28:21.180
That's the hope for that discussion. We will

01:28:21.934 --> 01:28:26.029
primarily— departments will come and we'll schedule

01:28:26.529 --> 01:28:29.400
it. It's gonna be a day. It's gonna be a listening opportunity for all of

01:28:29.900 --> 01:28:33.223
us. But they're going to be going over some of the accomplishments that they've done

01:28:33.336 --> 01:28:35.536
in that current or last fiscal year.

01:28:36.773 --> 01:28:40.290
They're going to be talking to you about the challenges, the barriers that they're facing

01:28:40.644 --> 01:28:44.017
as a department as well. And highlight

01:28:44.517 --> 01:28:48.322
things that I want to— that we— I hope that we spend more time on

01:28:48.498 --> 01:28:52.144
is that discussion of like where we are in our current level

01:28:52.644 --> 01:28:55.437
of service and looking at that by department.

01:28:56.223 --> 01:28:59.798
And I hope that that will provide some context

01:28:59.974 --> 01:29:04.206
for their budget requests and why they're requesting those resources.

01:29:05.184 --> 01:29:08.567
And the next one is to

01:29:09.067 --> 01:29:12.238
give the council the idea of like the trade-offs of like, if you

01:29:12.398 --> 01:29:15.444
approve those requests, what will happen to the level

01:29:15.944 --> 01:29:19.548
of service that they're proposing? Or if you don't approve those requests,

01:29:20.270 --> 01:29:24.167
what is the community or the council will expect in

01:29:24.200 --> 01:29:28.098
terms of the impact to the service level that they're currently

01:29:28.114 --> 01:29:31.900
handling. And part of that discussion of that continuous

01:29:32.400 --> 01:29:36.120
improvement, that we want to make sure that we can measure

01:29:36.216 --> 01:29:39.408
our success when it comes to the service level

01:29:39.505 --> 01:29:43.660
impacts, and how do we quantify that and explain

01:29:43.692 --> 01:29:47.510
that to, to the community as well. And I think that's just

01:29:48.010 --> 01:29:51.836
the, um, in the previous, next future budget planning,

01:29:52.189 --> 01:29:55.794
that's the time that as they talk about the accomplishment, that they can talk

01:29:56.294 --> 01:29:59.607
about the outcomes of like those budget decisions that we've done,

01:29:59.719 --> 01:30:02.988
you guys have done in that current fiscal

01:30:03.488 --> 01:30:07.298
year, what happened because of those budget decisions and what value they add

01:30:07.907 --> 01:30:11.224
to the community in general. So that's pretty much the Budget Congress

01:30:12.057 --> 01:30:14.845
kind of preview of what will that discussion will happen.

01:30:16.145 --> 01:30:19.756
And then we met with the communication department when

01:30:20.256 --> 01:30:24.700
it comes to the budget communication plan. They already created

01:30:24.732 --> 01:30:27.813
a dedicated budget webpage. So after this

01:30:27.846 --> 01:30:31.393
meeting, we're going to be posting some budget process information there. We'll put

01:30:31.893 --> 01:30:35.533
some budget timeline over there. Any communication or information

01:30:35.630 --> 01:30:39.321
that we may have, we'll probably put it on the website. Not just the

01:30:39.821 --> 01:30:43.509
website, because maybe Councilmember Clark, do you have a question? Okay,

01:30:43.606 --> 01:30:47.729
I'll finish first. Aside from a dedicated budget webpage,

01:30:48.226 --> 01:30:51.002
they're gonna put some information on the newsletter,

01:30:51.371 --> 01:30:54.660
their email blast, of course social media.

01:30:55.366 --> 01:30:59.601
And every after our budget work meetings

01:30:59.698 --> 01:31:03.227
like this or the future ones, we're going to be working with

01:31:03.727 --> 01:31:08.958
the communication department to come up with some infographics or some summary information

01:31:09.458 --> 01:31:11.834
that we can share to the public of where we are in the budget planning

01:31:11.866 --> 01:31:15.718
process. I know that there was some— it was

01:31:15.993 --> 01:31:19.695
brought up through those feedback meetings that there's a

01:31:20.195 --> 01:31:23.640
proposal of sending mailers with the budget dates to

01:31:24.140 --> 01:31:27.631
the community. I understand that there will be some costs associated

01:31:27.696 --> 01:31:31.396
by sending those mailers, so that's

01:31:31.958 --> 01:31:35.137
As a body, that's probably a direction that we probably want to hear from

01:31:35.217 --> 01:31:39.247
you guys. If you want to do that, mailers, or do you think posting it

01:31:39.312 --> 01:31:43.101
on a webpage, on our newsletter, email blast, and social

01:31:43.601 --> 01:31:47.372
media might be good enough, or enough for— as

01:31:47.420 --> 01:31:50.214
a way to broadcast this timeline and process?

01:31:51.515 --> 01:31:54.935
I think online is fine, but maybe I'm getting outvoted on that.

01:31:57.793 --> 01:32:02.273
I think that we saw through our Truth in Taxation and this year's

01:32:02.433 --> 01:32:06.271
budget that there are people who just aren't only online

01:32:06.303 --> 01:32:09.514
or don't know how to access it. Because even though we have a

01:32:10.014 --> 01:32:13.609
600-page budget that we publish and we do all of that stuff,

01:32:13.930 --> 01:32:17.029
there were still residents saying that it wasn't transparent enough.

01:32:17.607 --> 01:32:20.802
And I think if we were to do a mailer, it's probably going to cost

01:32:20.914 --> 01:32:24.864
a couple thousand dollars, even if it's

01:32:25.364 --> 01:32:28.808
postcard just saying, hey, we've heard you and here's our plan for this year.

01:32:28.889 --> 01:32:32.562
We want you to weigh in and be involved. We could also tell them

01:32:32.691 --> 01:32:36.077
about the survey and other things. I agree we should

01:32:36.577 --> 01:32:39.285
be judicious with what we send and what we spend,

01:32:40.210 --> 01:32:43.004
but for this specific thing, I think it's important.

01:32:44.510 --> 01:32:48.486
Councilmember Hughes. Yeah, so I

01:32:48.535 --> 01:32:52.004
think Online is, is fine.

01:32:52.844 --> 01:32:54.992
The challenge that we had in communicating is,

01:32:56.623 --> 01:33:00.229
one, we were trying to leverage a medium, the Eagle Mountain Citizens page,

01:33:00.583 --> 01:33:04.440
which people roll through. So you might make one comment

01:33:04.940 --> 01:33:08.725
and then you feel like you've satisfied the question,

01:33:08.789 --> 01:33:12.024
but then that same question comes in 10 different posts.

01:33:12.687 --> 01:33:16.375
So there wasn't one strategic place that I could point to

01:33:16.875 --> 01:33:22.317
and say, here it is, here's The 5 to 10 page document

01:33:22.349 --> 01:33:26.120
that you can go get a very good summary. We did do a very high-level

01:33:26.136 --> 01:33:29.457
budget and brief that was reactionary, but I don't think it had

01:33:29.957 --> 01:33:33.208
enough depth to really answer the question. So I'm okay

01:33:33.273 --> 01:33:36.698
with online. I don't know that we need to mail something out to every

01:33:37.373 --> 01:33:40.506
resident. That would be pretty costly, but we could

01:33:41.006 --> 01:33:44.931
do a communication campaign to direct everyone to the— webpage

01:33:44.979 --> 01:33:46.695
to download a useful,

01:33:47.609 --> 01:33:51.378
well-documented, drafted budget in brief. Our regular

01:33:51.878 --> 01:33:55.308
budget is too big and too vague.

01:33:55.468 --> 01:33:59.366
It didn't really break out who was being hired. You'd had

01:33:59.398 --> 01:34:02.686
to kind of sift through the current year budget and the next

01:34:03.186 --> 01:34:05.733
year budget to figure out, oh, it looks like they're hiring someone here.

01:34:06.968 --> 01:34:10.673
So those would be my comments. I think if we make a really good budget

01:34:10.706 --> 01:34:14.589
in brief, we could hand them out at the The open house,

01:34:15.022 --> 01:34:18.844
that would be fine to have some paper copies there, and then

01:34:19.052 --> 01:34:21.509
the rest of it we, we could direct online and then just,

01:34:22.569 --> 01:34:25.700
uh, through other means direct people there when they

01:34:26.200 --> 01:34:28.863
have questions. So I think the quality of the budget and brief is the most

01:34:29.363 --> 01:34:32.460
important piece here for me. Um, I, I agree with

01:34:32.960 --> 01:34:35.398
you, Councilmember Huebsch, but I, but I'm gonna just throw a little caveat in here.

01:34:35.575 --> 01:34:39.958
I don't think it costs us anything if we just put little— well,

01:34:40.022 --> 01:34:42.977
it's going to cost something, but I think it's very minimal if we just put

01:34:43.041 --> 01:34:46.862
a little QR code with

01:34:47.135 --> 01:34:50.507
an email address or an address. There you go. People can access it in their

01:34:51.007 --> 01:34:54.184
utility billing, or we even print it at the bottom of the utility billing page.

01:34:54.280 --> 01:34:57.395
Just something that directs people to where to find the information regarding

01:34:57.492 --> 01:35:00.799
the budget. And I think we— I really think the

01:35:01.299 --> 01:35:03.979
QR code on a postcard is— I mean,

01:35:04.894 --> 01:35:07.832
If no one else wants to pay for it, then obviously that's fine. But it's

01:35:07.929 --> 01:35:11.349
not expensive, especially when you have it sent to every address

01:35:11.798 --> 01:35:15.572
within a certain zip code, because your rate goes down quite

01:35:16.072 --> 01:35:19.088
a bit. So like, maybe we should look at the dollar amount and see what

01:35:19.588 --> 01:35:22.781
it is. But I think if we do like a kickoff postcard to help people

01:35:23.281 --> 01:35:26.073
know this is where the budget stuff is, that might be helpful. And I think

01:35:26.573 --> 01:35:29.139
this is what I'm thinking and the reason I think that the utility billing makes

01:35:29.639 --> 01:35:33.103
the most sense. Because if someone is still requesting a paper utility billing,

01:35:34.193 --> 01:35:37.530
now you've contacted them. You've given them the information.

01:35:37.627 --> 01:35:41.814
And if they're already doing it electronically online, then they're already accessing the information

01:35:42.314 --> 01:35:45.953
online. I think that's a pretty good measuring stick to

01:35:45.969 --> 01:35:49.178
reach the right people that you're concerned about, Councilmember Clark. And I think

01:35:49.194 --> 01:35:52.563
it also saves us some resourcing. Well, and I think

01:35:52.707 --> 01:35:55.306
just to weigh in, looking back at last year,

01:35:56.413 --> 01:36:00.310
It's something worth trying for this reason. We went to cottage meetings,

01:36:00.422 --> 01:36:04.111
we went to everything and tried to talk about it. We were online, we had

01:36:04.611 --> 01:36:08.617
web pages, we had handouts, we went to the parks, and it wasn't until

01:36:08.842 --> 01:36:12.579
everybody received their mailer from the county that all of a sudden,

01:36:13.413 --> 01:36:16.909
what's happening? And I'm thinking, what do you mean what's happening?

01:36:17.342 --> 01:36:19.956
I tried to beg you to talk to me about taxes.

01:36:21.977 --> 01:36:25.253
You know, if there's a way to— if that's

01:36:25.349 --> 01:36:28.624
the way people see it, I don't know. We're already paying

01:36:28.897 --> 01:36:32.109
to print and to send those utility bills anyway.

01:36:32.350 --> 01:36:35.721
I don't see that as a significant increase in cost to do

01:36:36.221 --> 01:36:38.435
that. I'm with Councilmember Wright on this, and the reason why they were up in

01:36:38.935 --> 01:36:42.851
arms is because they saw a proposed bill, like their proposed

01:36:43.351 --> 01:36:46.608
payment going up. That's the reason why they were up in arms about it.

01:36:47.202 --> 01:36:50.026
But I think you put it— I guess what we could do is we could

01:36:50.526 --> 01:36:54.085
evaluate the cost both ways. Let's get the

01:36:54.585 --> 01:36:57.358
data on the table. Because it is only a couple thousand dollars.

01:36:57.855 --> 01:37:02.492
Okay, that's different than if it's $20,000. Yeah, it's for sure not $20,000.

01:37:03.952 --> 01:37:07.209
More than half of our residents opt out of paper statements.

01:37:07.353 --> 01:37:10.754
Okay, but I do— I personally,

01:37:10.867 --> 01:37:15.179
in terms of our finance budgeting, I would really like to see a—

01:37:16.076 --> 01:37:19.233
knowing again, when people receive their notice, I'm supportive of trying to do a

01:37:19.345 --> 01:37:23.094
postcard in advance of the open house that says,

01:37:23.527 --> 01:37:26.619
we've done it, we've adopted the tentative budget. Here's the postcard that has

01:37:26.651 --> 01:37:29.759
the QR code where you can go to get more information. Here's the date for

01:37:30.259 --> 01:37:32.739
the open house that we invite you to come to find out more stuff.

01:37:33.300 --> 01:37:36.745
But I do think there's value in at least trying to do that.

01:37:37.146 --> 01:37:39.805
And if we do it this year and we don't get any more engagement,

01:37:39.853 --> 01:37:43.415
then we don't need to do it again next year. But I personally would like

01:37:43.479 --> 01:37:46.706
to try that. One other thing too,

01:37:46.915 --> 01:37:50.945
we could spend maybe like $400 or $500 on 2-sided

01:37:51.445 --> 01:37:55.537
yard signs and put some of those up with a QR code around strategic

01:37:55.602 --> 01:37:59.343
places like where schools and heavily traveled intersections

01:37:59.760 --> 01:38:03.132
and just say questions about the city budget, you know,

01:38:03.148 --> 01:38:06.713
something like that. It would be a very inexpensive way to catch

01:38:07.213 --> 01:38:10.622
people people and get them to pay attention, especially if

01:38:10.638 --> 01:38:14.015
we put it up at a targeted time and not forever. Are we

01:38:14.515 --> 01:38:15.937
exempt from the snipe sign code?

01:38:19.010 --> 01:38:22.482
Yes, we are, because we have a mayor that could, um,

01:38:22.839 --> 01:38:25.093
what is it called when he like forgives you?

01:38:26.670 --> 01:38:30.112
Pardon us? We can get a pardon. Yeah, I mean, because we obviously will

01:38:30.145 --> 01:38:33.063
put it on our message boards and, you know, like in things like that.

01:38:33.907 --> 01:38:37.777
And do as much as we can. What we'll

01:38:38.277 --> 01:38:40.169
do is we'll get some pricing, we'll bring it back to the council so that

01:38:40.669 --> 01:38:44.216
you can see what it would cost and if that's something you would be supportive

01:38:44.716 --> 01:38:47.780
of. 'Cause what I'm hearing is there's a desire to make sure that we're communicating

01:38:47.812 --> 01:38:51.056
more effectively. That's what I'm hearing. And so help us evaluate

01:38:51.248 --> 01:38:54.685
what it's gonna cost us to do that through the different means and methods that

01:38:54.701 --> 01:38:57.077
we've talked about. Yes, we wanna increase our level of service.

01:38:59.117 --> 01:39:02.416
There you go. All right, this is the last slide, I promise.

01:39:03.321 --> 01:39:06.895
Overall, just to summarize all the things that we've talked about and the

01:39:06.960 --> 01:39:10.335
things that we will be talking about in the future, I look at it in

01:39:10.367 --> 01:39:13.933
this way, wherein it will depend on how

01:39:13.997 --> 01:39:17.483
much impact you would like to make in the community and to balance that

01:39:17.983 --> 01:39:20.207
to the budget that we have. And sometimes we may not be able to do

01:39:20.707 --> 01:39:24.415
it this year, but I know that as we continue doing this strategic planning

01:39:24.610 --> 01:39:28.035
and Long-term planning, it will help us get

01:39:28.535 --> 01:39:32.518
there too. So for now, do you have any questions

01:39:32.695 --> 01:39:36.202
in general about all the things that we talked about? The next time that

01:39:36.234 --> 01:39:39.737
we'll be meeting, we'll be discussing public safety on December

01:39:40.090 --> 01:39:43.721
8th. Mayor,

01:39:43.785 --> 01:39:46.438
can we take 60 seconds and have the people that are sitting up here as

01:39:46.938 --> 01:39:50.614
elected officials just spout off all the things that we get asked the most

01:39:51.114 --> 01:39:54.187
about? Because I think those are the things that we could start doing education now.

01:39:54.958 --> 01:39:57.721
Like, when we've gone to meetings, when people have emailed us,

01:39:58.251 --> 01:40:01.479
like, 60 seconds, all of us just say the different ones.

01:40:01.543 --> 01:40:05.060
Because I feel like there's— we should

01:40:05.076 --> 01:40:08.449
start targeting education. I think tax rates,

01:40:08.690 --> 01:40:11.902
the difference between impact fees and how

01:40:12.063 --> 01:40:15.821
growth should be paying for itself, and explaining that.

01:40:15.917 --> 01:40:19.917
What other things have you guys heard? I don't think anybody ever on

01:40:20.417 --> 01:40:22.879
this council has done anything in 60 seconds. It took you longer than that to

01:40:22.911 --> 01:40:26.802
explain it. So start

01:40:27.302 --> 01:40:30.404
with you and let's then— She was— I know education.

01:40:32.573 --> 01:40:35.678
This is the time period where there's not a lot of information in the budget,

01:40:36.032 --> 01:40:39.619
but we could be tasking our comms team or even

01:40:39.732 --> 01:40:44.648
us as elected officials to put information out in a clear

01:40:45.148 --> 01:40:48.128
But I think coordinating it a little bit better than how we had to do

01:40:48.144 --> 01:40:51.785
it with our different efforts

01:40:51.913 --> 01:40:55.618
through this last year, I think, is good. Craig, you've campaigned recently.

01:40:55.762 --> 01:40:58.264
Like, what, what are some of the things that you got asked the most?

01:40:58.568 --> 01:41:01.327
Well, I'll answer that one in just a second, but I think, for instance,

01:41:01.615 --> 01:41:05.513
communicating to the public the different buckets of money and

01:41:05.625 --> 01:41:08.415
what's restricted and what's not restricted. I think that would be helpful.

01:41:08.594 --> 01:41:11.779
So they can understand what we're talking about relative to

01:41:15.960 --> 01:41:19.400
Let's see. I'm trying to think. I mean, public safety

01:41:19.900 --> 01:41:23.295
issues relative to streets. I mean, that's one of the things I think comes up

01:41:23.795 --> 01:41:26.980
very frequently. Safety in school zones.

01:41:29.379 --> 01:41:32.502
Quality— like why parks might be looking the

01:41:33.002 --> 01:41:35.596
way they are. I mean, why open space is not being taken care of.

01:41:36.155 --> 01:41:40.114
It really comes down to lack of resources sometimes on our side with employees

01:41:40.147 --> 01:41:41.988
and such. We don't have enough staffing.

01:41:44.700 --> 01:41:47.833
Brett, how about you? One of the things that I wish our citizens

01:41:47.865 --> 01:41:51.208
understood more clearly is just taxes,

01:41:51.305 --> 01:41:54.717
how they're— where they're created

01:41:54.863 --> 01:41:58.340
and what they fund. I mean, what they're tied to. Property taxes,

01:41:58.582 --> 01:42:02.244
everybody complains about property tax, and property tax is one of the smallest revenue

01:42:02.261 --> 01:42:06.003
streams that we deal with, right? I would like our people to understand

01:42:06.503 --> 01:42:10.485
property tax, municipal energy tax, what, what generates them, what is

01:42:10.597 --> 01:42:13.167
it that creates that revenue stream, and sales tax.

01:42:14.131 --> 01:42:17.777
I think if they understood that a little more clearly, that we'd have more people

01:42:17.809 --> 01:42:21.375
that would shop in Eagle Mountain. They understand that if you shop outside of Eagle

01:42:21.875 --> 01:42:25.600
Mountain, 50% of your tax stays in the municipality that you purchase your product

01:42:26.100 --> 01:42:30.038
from. If you buy it on Amazon, Money stays here, 100% of

01:42:30.070 --> 01:42:33.463
it. I don't think our people really understand that. It's not something I understood until

01:42:33.963 --> 01:42:35.256
I sat in this seat, and I'm old.

01:42:37.597 --> 01:42:40.877
I think if we understood that, that we would be a little more protective

01:42:41.377 --> 01:42:44.848
of our own environment and say, gosh, I can fill my truck up

01:42:45.394 --> 01:42:48.544
at $180 worth of diesel

01:42:49.044 --> 01:42:50.488
fuel. I can fill it up in Eagle Mountain, or I can fill it up

01:42:50.988 --> 01:42:52.272
in Lehi. Where should I fill it up? I always fill it up in Eagle

01:42:52.772 --> 01:42:56.549
Mountain. up in Eagle Mountain for that very reason, because those taxes stay here

01:42:57.543 --> 01:43:01.501
and it benefits my community. So I just, I think

01:43:02.001 --> 01:43:04.770
if our people understood that, they would be like, oh, okay, it might cost me

01:43:05.270 --> 01:43:08.744
another nickel in Eagle Mountain, which it doesn't, by the way, but it might

01:43:08.856 --> 01:43:12.205
on any given day. But those revenues

01:43:12.705 --> 01:43:16.243
stay home. And I think that's important for us to understand. Mayor,

01:43:16.292 --> 01:43:18.839
how about you? What are some of the things you get beat up a lot

01:43:21.264 --> 01:43:24.620
Every day's a new ask. I mean, I'm just thinking about—

01:43:25.037 --> 01:43:26.530
Ask, A-S-K.

01:43:27.285 --> 01:43:31.861
I'm like, what did I say? But they're

01:43:31.877 --> 01:43:35.313
always asking something. And what are we

01:43:35.813 --> 01:43:39.087
doing about this? And it's just what fire are we going to? But yeah,

01:43:39.167 --> 01:43:43.020
I mean, the big conversations this year were around the taxes and traffic.

01:43:44.968 --> 01:43:48.784
Sometimes I get excited and, hey, what have you done? And just

01:43:48.816 --> 01:43:51.472
kind of a report on what's going on. Like, you mean this week or last

01:43:51.972 --> 01:43:55.607
week? 'Cause we're on a whole new, you know, it just changes

01:43:56.107 --> 01:43:59.769
every day. But definitely open space, our parks

01:43:59.801 --> 01:44:03.232
maintenance, taxes, transportation. Yeah, I mean

01:44:03.732 --> 01:44:07.104
all of that. Mayor. I've got another one to throw at

01:44:07.604 --> 01:44:11.098
you real quick. I think it would be really helpful,

01:44:11.326 --> 01:44:13.560
and I emailed Evan about this,

01:44:14.503 --> 01:44:17.762
He liked this idea too. If we could have somewhere on our website showing

01:44:18.260 --> 01:44:22.210
here's how much money we have received from data centers or are

01:44:22.710 --> 01:44:25.678
receiving from data centers, a running tally that people go, hey, take a look,

01:44:26.208 --> 01:44:29.740
the data centers paid this, this, this, this. And if we had something

01:44:29.821 --> 01:44:33.755
that was just a running tally that is just there, it should be transparent

01:44:34.255 --> 01:44:36.966
anyway. I think that'd be very helpful. We can point them directly to that.

01:44:37.287 --> 01:44:39.615
It'd be sweet if we could— you know how they have the debt clock?

01:44:39.872 --> 01:44:43.324
Yes. The federal debt clock, how it's like— And then

01:44:43.824 --> 01:44:47.322
people can see, oh my goodness. Yeah, compared to the $6 a

01:44:47.339 --> 01:44:50.453
year we used to get for that land. Yeah, people— so I think— and that's

01:44:50.953 --> 01:44:54.163
something I think we could put together on the communications side that would be helpful.

01:44:54.708 --> 01:44:58.161
I think to add to that, we ought to show the other entities and

01:44:58.661 --> 01:45:01.838
what they're getting from the data centers too, because even though we might be giving

01:45:01.870 --> 01:45:04.728
some incentive from the city, we're bringing a lot of money into the schools,

01:45:04.824 --> 01:45:07.789
that type of thing. I totally agree, but I think, I, I think it'd be—

01:45:08.248 --> 01:45:10.018
talking about communications, let's put that together.

01:45:12.470 --> 01:45:15.745
Council Member Hughes? Yeah, I agree.

01:45:15.956 --> 01:45:19.434
I think these are all great ideas. So it sounds

01:45:19.934 --> 01:45:23.737
like a place where residents can learn about how property tax works,

01:45:23.883 --> 01:45:27.647
sales tax, MET, personal property.

01:45:28.177 --> 01:45:31.698
Those are things that I'm just learning this year. I'm like

01:45:32.198 --> 01:45:34.854
Brett, I've lived here for a long time, and you all were there when I

01:45:35.855 --> 01:45:39.812
had the epiphany around that property taxes dollar amounts

01:45:40.312 --> 01:45:43.844
stay fixed. So any education we can

01:45:44.344 --> 01:45:46.014
do around that is a, is a net positive.

01:45:48.287 --> 01:45:51.004
I mean, Council Member Huebsch, I remember when— I mean,

01:45:51.667 --> 01:45:55.258
someone's always frustrated with us, period. I mean, you just— if you're going to

01:45:55.758 --> 01:45:58.085
be an elected official, accept that as the reality.

01:45:58.532 --> 01:46:02.402
Okay, but I remember when we had some residents up near where

01:46:02.902 --> 01:46:06.320
the new Walmart is at, their frustrations, and they just couldn't believe that

01:46:06.820 --> 01:46:09.675
we would consider putting a Walmart there and da da da da da da fill

01:46:10.175 --> 01:46:14.509
in the blank, right? And as I looked at that, and I certainly heard

01:46:15.009 --> 01:46:18.410
them and understood some of their concerns, and we fought really hard with those council

01:46:18.442 --> 01:46:20.867
members that were on the council at that time to do all we could to

01:46:21.367 --> 01:46:24.801
raise the quality of the of the architectural elements of that. that site,

01:46:24.865 --> 01:46:29.088
and I personally think it's one of the nicest Walmarts in the state. It is.

01:46:29.907 --> 01:46:33.263
And we got beat up on both sides of that equation,

01:46:33.295 --> 01:46:36.731
but here was the thing that really kind of pushed me to

01:46:37.231 --> 01:46:40.568
the Walmart side of things, and there are those that will still gripe at

01:46:40.600 --> 01:46:44.502
me for saying this. But at the time, the sales tax revenues

01:46:44.775 --> 01:46:48.276
that were anticipated per year to be generated from that one

01:46:48.372 --> 01:46:51.960
store were about $1 million. Million of additional

01:46:52.234 --> 01:46:55.761
sales tax revenue. And so what did that mean? That didn't mean

01:46:55.794 --> 01:46:58.666
that we were going to take that million dollars of sales tax revenue and,

01:46:58.849 --> 01:47:01.126
and, and split it with Saratoga Springs because,

01:47:02.837 --> 01:47:06.693
because the sales occurred in their city boundary. It meant that those sales

01:47:07.193 --> 01:47:10.323
happened here and we kept the full sales tax revenue.

01:47:11.388 --> 01:47:13.486
I mean, you got to send half of it to the state and then you

01:47:13.986 --> 01:47:16.796
get your proportion back based on your population. That happens no matter where you buy

01:47:17.296 --> 01:47:20.678
it, but the other 50% stays local. It stays in your economy. So $500,000 of

01:47:21.178 --> 01:47:25.011
that comes into Eagle Mountain's coffers and will every year. And if

01:47:25.511 --> 01:47:28.590
the cells grow, then it— then, then that grows. And so for me, that was

01:47:28.622 --> 01:47:32.168
one of the motivating factors in saying yes to that, but trying to

01:47:32.217 --> 01:47:36.052
protect the impact to the residents. And that's the balance that we face as

01:47:36.068 --> 01:47:39.021
we deal with budget items, land approvals,

01:47:39.855 --> 01:47:43.290
data centers that may want to come. That's always the balance. And I,

01:47:43.611 --> 01:47:47.314
I think that most of our citizens They

01:47:47.814 --> 01:47:51.665
find themselves on one side or the other of most of these arguments. Those who

01:47:51.941 --> 01:47:55.553
think we're a bunch of knuckleheads, I wish they'd just give us a little grace

01:47:55.843 --> 01:47:58.855
and sit down and really evaluate the data. They can still disagree with us.

01:47:58.984 --> 01:48:02.173
That's okay. They should, and some of them should, because if everybody agrees with you,

01:48:02.350 --> 01:48:03.107
you're in the wrong place.

01:48:06.445 --> 01:48:11.150
I wish they could understand just how tax

01:48:11.199 --> 01:48:13.578
revenue works. and how it impacts them,

01:48:14.744 --> 01:48:18.550
um, and how cities are funded. We don't have some magic bucket

01:48:19.050 --> 01:48:21.369
of money that we get to tap into every time you need something.

01:48:23.186 --> 01:48:25.576
You should just take it out of my pocket because apparently they're lined.

01:48:27.340 --> 01:48:30.794
Well, and it's, it's probably important to note too that as far as sales taxes

01:48:30.826 --> 01:48:34.330
are concerned, it didn't raise anything to the residents. They would

01:48:34.830 --> 01:48:38.272
have spent that money anyway. It just would have benefited another community instead.

01:48:43.257 --> 01:48:46.969
Thanks, Mayor. Okay, we do have a couple items to

01:48:47.469 --> 01:48:50.856
talk about in a closed session. Is there anything else? Brad, I, I know

01:48:51.356 --> 01:48:54.067
you were kind of— were you waiting to weigh in on anything? Okay,

01:48:54.890 --> 01:48:58.645
you're good. Uh, if that's everything, we do

01:48:59.145 --> 01:49:01.223
have some items to talk about in closed session if you'd wish.

01:49:02.800 --> 01:49:06.450
I'll make the motion, Mayor. I move to adjourn to a closed session for the

01:49:06.950 --> 01:49:10.737
purpose of discussion of pending or reasonably imminent

01:49:10.769 --> 01:49:14.141
litigation, the character, professional competence, or physical or mental health of an

01:49:14.641 --> 01:49:18.508
individual, the purchase, sale, lease, or exchange of real property, a proposed development

01:49:19.008 --> 01:49:22.714
agreement, a project proposal, or financing proposal related to

01:49:22.746 --> 01:49:26.600
the development of land owned by the state or a political subdivision, and/or the

01:49:27.100 --> 01:49:30.375
deployment of security personnel, devices, or systems pursuant to Section

01:49:30.875 --> 01:49:34.356
52-4-205 of the Utah

01:49:34.485 --> 01:49:38.223
Code Annotated. Second. Okay, we have a motion by Councilmember

01:49:38.723 --> 01:49:41.632
Wright and a second by Councilmember Clark. Councilmember Hewish? Yes.

01:49:42.165 --> 01:49:45.606
Councilmember Clark? Yes. Councilmember Wright? Yes. Councilmember Whiting? Yes.

01:49:45.832 --> 01:49:48.423
Okay, we'll adjourn to closed session at 7:48,

01:49:49.367 --> 01:49:52.246
and we will have to come back here to adjourn the meeting, just so everyone's

01:50:45.103 --> 01:50:48.973
I want someone to sing. All right, it is 8:42.

01:50:49.198 --> 01:50:52.615
We're back in our regular meeting, and I will— Second.

01:50:53.080 --> 01:50:56.525
I'll take a motion from Melissa to adjourn, a second from— sorry,

01:50:56.573 --> 01:50:59.394
Councilmember Clark and Councilmember Wright. All in favor? Aye.

01:51:00.131 --> 01:51:03.576
No one opposed? We are adjourned at 8:42.
